\begin{abstract}
We give a complete structural analysis of a family of proposed integer-factoring
procedures built on the Berggren ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples.
Two concrete proposals motivate the study: a \emph{tree sieve}, which harvests
leg pairs $(m_i,n_i)$ from the tree and arranges $\prod_i (m_i-n_i)(m_i+n_i)=Y^2$
in the hope that a gcd splits a semiprime $N$; and a \emph{multi-target
relaxation}, which replaces the exact search goal $a=N$ by the far weaker goal
$\gcd(a,N)>1$.

We prove that the tree sieve is invalid as stated: the relation it produces is an
identity in $\Z$, never a congruence modulo $N$, so the gcd step returns $N$
itself and the relation is divisible by every modulus. We then quantify the
``lottery'' behaviour of any $N$-independent candidate generator: $k$ tickets
$D_0,\dots,D_{k-1}$ win on at most $\sum_i \log_2 D_i$ primes of any pool, so
success probabilities add linearly and there is no amplification. A
breadth-first search of the ternary tree is starved: reaching a hypotenuse of
size $V$ requires expanding $n\ge\sqrt{V/5}$ nodes.

The central positive contribution is a \emph{blindness} theorem. Every prime
divisor of the hypotenuse of a primitive Pythagorean triple is $\equiv 1
\pmod 4$; hence the tree's hypotenuse face has gcd $1$ with every modulus whose
prime factors are all $\equiv 3\pmod 4$ --- zero winning tickets, uniformly over
the entire infinite tree. This simultaneously explains the empirically measured
smoothness advantage of tree values (a halved factor base) and kills the method
on a positive-density class of moduli. We generalise: if $c=a^2+Db^2$ with
$(a,b)$ primitive, then $-D$ is a square modulo every prime divisor of $c$, so
any search constrained to a fixed norm form is blind to that form's inert primes.
The blind classes for $D=1$ and $D=2$ are incomparable. By Dirichlet, each blind
class contains infinitely many composite moduli.

For the multi-target relaxation we prove that the least $a\ge 2$ with
$\gcd(a,N)>1$ is \emph{exactly} $\min(p,q)$ for $N=pq$, so an ascending value
sweep first hits at $\min(p,q)$ deterministically; its cost is $\min(p,q)\le
\sqrt N$, the trial-division exponent; and the speedup over the exact target is
exactly $\max(p,q)\in[\sqrt N,N/2]$. A no-free-lunch theorem shows no
$N$-independent reordering of candidates escapes. All of this assembles into a
trichotomy: any splitter reading values off the integer face of the Berggren tree
operates in exactly one of three regimes --- integer square identity (returns
$N$, no split), genuine mod-$N$ congruence of squares (Dixon/quadratic-sieve
class), or ascending value sweep (trial division). Every route ends in a known
method, and none beats Pollard's rho.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} integer factorisation; Pythagorean triples; Berggren
tree; congruence of squares; binary quadratic forms; norm-form blindness; trial
division; Pollard rho; lottery bounds.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The proposals}

Integer factorisation of a semiprime $N=pq$ underlies a large fraction of
deployed public-key cryptography. Practical subexponential algorithms --- Dixon's
random squares, the quadratic sieve, the number field sieve --- all realise the
same core mechanism: find integers $x,y$ with
\[
  x^2\equiv y^2 \pmod N,\qquad x\not\equiv \pm y\pmod N,
\]
and read off $\gcd(x-y,N)$ as a proper factor. The difficulty is entirely in
manufacturing the congruence; the gcd is free.

Because the mechanism consumes \emph{squares}, it is perennially tempting to feed
it objects that come pre-equipped with square relations. The most classical such
object is the set of Pythagorean triples. Berggren's theorem states that
repeatedly applying the three maps
\begin{align*}
  (a,b,c)&\mapsto (a-2b+2c,\;2a-b+2c,\;2a-2b+3c),\\
  (a,b,c)&\mapsto (a+2b+2c,\;2a+b+2c,\;2a+2b+3c),\\
  (a,b,c)&\mapsto (-a+2b+2c,\;-2a+b+2c,\;-2a+2b+3c),
\end{align*}
starting from the root $(3,4,5)$ enumerates every primitive Pythagorean triple
exactly once: an infinite ternary tree, all of whose nodes satisfy
$a^2+b^2=c^2$ identically.

\paragraph{Proposal A (tree sieve).} Harvest leg pairs $(m_i,n_i)$ from tree
nodes and select a subset so that $\prod_i (m_i-n_i)(m_i+n_i)=Y^2$ is a perfect
square; form a relation between squares and compute $\gcd(X-Y,N)$.

\paragraph{Proposal B (multi-target relaxation).} Run a tree search whose
objective is a node value equal to $N$; observing that this is astronomically
unlikely, relax the objective to a node value $a$ with $\gcd(a,N)>1$, and use a
value-guided best-first search rather than a blind FIFO queue.

\subsection{The empirical record}

Both proposals were implemented and measured. \emph{Proposal A}: end-to-end,
$8$ splits in $12000$ trials, against a baseline of $4$ splits in $12000$ trials
for random gcds, against a heuristic per-trial prediction of $6.55\times10^{-4}$;
tree-derived values measured $7.31\times$ smoother than random integers of
comparable size, against a naive prediction of ${\sim}44\times$; breadth-first
search with $50\,000$ expanded nodes never reached the intended analysis window.
\emph{Proposal B}: blind FIFO search had $55\%$ of runs censored with fitted cost
exponent $\alpha=1.17$ on the finished subset; value-guided best-first search won
$1500$ of $1500$ paired trials, median visit ratio $0.111$, zero censoring --- a
speedup of roughly $10^{12}$, converting a ${\sim}2^{56}$-unit search into a
${\sim}2^{16}$-unit one. However, $100\%$ of first hits occurred at exactly
$a=\min(p,q)$, with fitted exponent $\alpha=1.087$ and $r^2=1.0$.

\subsection{Contributions}

This paper explains all of these numbers and shows in each case that the
phenomenon is \emph{forced} rather than incidental: \S2 invalidity as stated;
\S3 the lottery bound; \S4 breadth-first starvation; \S5 hypotenuse-face
blindness; \S6 norm-form blindness in general; \S7 the multi-target relaxation as
trial division; \S8 no free lunch for search orders; \S9 the trichotomy and its
non-vacuous positive half. Throughout, $p,q$ denote primes, $N=pq$ a semiprime,
and $r$ an arbitrary prime divisor.

\section{Obstruction I: an identity in \texorpdfstring{$\Z$}{Z} carries no information modulo \texorpdfstring{$N$}{N}}

The distinguishing feature of Proposal A is that $N$ appears nowhere in the
construction of the relation; it is introduced only at the last step. This is
fatal.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqeq}
If $X,Y\in\Z$ with $X\ge 0$, $Y\ge 0$ and $X^2=Y^2$, then $X=Y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(X-Y)(X+Y)=0$, so $X=Y$ or $X=-Y$; in the second case $X+Y=0$ with both
nonnegative forces $X=Y=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer square relations are vacuous]\label{thm:vacuous}
Let $X,Y\ge 0$ be integers with $X^2=Y^2$. Then $\gcd(X-Y,N)=|N|$ for every
integer $N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sqeq}, $X-Y=0$, and $\gcd(0,N)=|N|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No information]\label{cor:noinfo}
Under the same hypotheses, $M\mid X-Y$ for every integer $M$.
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:noinfo} is the sharpest phrasing of the defect: the relation
is divisible by \emph{every} modulus simultaneously, so it distinguishes nothing.
The gcd step, whose purpose is to extract the modulus-specific part of the
relation, receives an input from which the modulus has already been eliminated.
The contrast with the intended mechanism is exact.

\begin{theorem}[Dixon split]\label{thm:dixon}
Let $N>1$ and $x,y\in\Z$ satisfy $N\mid (x-y)(x+y)$, $N\nmid x-y$ and
$N\nmid x+y$. Then $1<\gcd(x-y,N)<N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $g=\gcd(x-y,N)$, so $g\mid N$. If $g=0$ then $x-y=0$ and $N\mid x-y$,
contradiction. If $g=1$ then $x-y$ and $N$ are coprime, so $N\mid (x-y)(x+y)$
gives $N\mid x+y$, contradiction; hence $g>1$. Finally $g\mid N$ gives $g\le N$,
and $g=N$ would give $N\mid x-y$, contradiction; hence $g<N$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:vacuous} and \ref{thm:dixon} form a dichotomy sharpened in
\S\ref{sec:tri}: a square relation is either an identity in $\Z$ (useless) or a
genuine congruence modulo $N$ with inequivalent roots (Dixon class). Any
correction of Proposal A that forces reduction modulo $N$ moves it, by
definition, into the quadratic-sieve family, whose cost is governed by smoothness
statistics, not by the tree.

\section{Obstruction II: \texorpdfstring{$N$}{N}-independent candidates form a lottery}

Suppose Proposal A is weakened to: emit integers $D_0,\dots,D_{k-1}$ derived from
the tree, and compute $\gcd(D_i,N)$ for each. The tree does not depend on $N$, so
the $D_i$ are fixed before $N$ is seen; each is a lottery ticket, winning against
a hidden prime $r$ exactly when $r\mid D_i$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:omega}
For $D\ge 1$, the number $\omega(D)$ of distinct prime factors satisfies
$\omega(D)\le\log_2 D$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$2^{\omega(D)}\le\prod_{r\mid D} r\le D$, since every prime is at least $2$ and
the radical divides $D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One ticket]\label{thm:oneticket}
Let $D\ne 0$ and let $S$ be a finite set of primes. Then
$\#\{r\in S: r\mid D\}\le\log_2|D|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The set injects into the distinct prime factors of $D$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:omega}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tickets add linearly]\label{thm:union}
Let $D_0,\dots,D_{k-1}$ be nonzero integers and $S$ a finite set of primes. Then
\[
  \#\{\,r\in S: \exists i,\ r\mid D_i\,\}\ \le\ \sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\log_2|D_i|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The winning set is contained in $\bigcup_i\{r\in S: r\mid D_i\}$; cardinality of a
union is at most the sum of cardinalities; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:oneticket}
termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Lottery probability bound]
If the hidden prime is drawn uniformly from a nonempty pool $S$, the probability
that some $D_i$ wins is at most $|S|^{-1}\sum_i\log_2|D_i|$.
\end{corollary}

The content of Theorem~\ref{thm:union} is the \emph{absence of amplification}.
For a hard semiprime the relevant pool is the primes near $\sqrt N$, of size
$|S|\asymp\sqrt N/\log N$, so with $\log_2|D_i|=O(\log N)$ the bound reads
$\Pr[\text{success}]=O(k\,N^{-1/2+o(1)})$: generic gcd luck, the same rate as
drawing $k$ random integers. Observing $8$ successes where a random baseline
gives $4$, at a per-trial rate of $6.55\times10^{-4}$, is exactly the linear-in-$k$
behaviour predicted, and is not evidence of a mechanism.

\section{Obstruction III: breadth-first starvation}

Let a \emph{triple} be $(a,b,c)\in\Z^3$, let $\mathrm{step}_i$ ($i\in\{0,1,2\}$)
be the three Berggren maps, and let $\berg(w)$ be the node reached from $(3,4,5)$
along a word $w\in\{0,1,2\}^*$. Call a triple \emph{admissible} if $0<a$, $0<b$,
$a<c$, $b<c$.

\begin{lemma}
Admissibility holds at the root and is preserved by each $\mathrm{step}_i$, as is
the Pythagorean relation $a^2+b^2=c^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Direct computation: each map sends $a^2+b^2-c^2$ to itself up to sign, and the
inequalities follow from positivity together with $a,b<c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Bounded growth]\label{lem:growth}
For an admissible triple with hypotenuse $c$, each child has hypotenuse at most
$7c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The child hypotenuses are $2a-2b+3c$, $2a+2b+3c$, $-2a+2b+3c$; under $0<a<c$ and
$0<b<c$ each is at most $2c+2c+3c=7c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Geometric ceiling]\label{prop:ceiling}
A node at depth $L$ has hypotenuse at most $5\cdot 7^L$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $L$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:growth}, starting from $c=5$.
\end{proof}

Write $\nodes(L)=\sum_{i=0}^{L}3^i$, so $3^L\le\nodes(L)$.

\begin{theorem}[BFS starvation]\label{thm:starve}
If breadth-first search reaches a node of depth $L$ whose hypotenuse is at least
$V$, and $n=\nodes(L)$ nodes have been expanded, then $V\le 5n^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$V\le 5\cdot 7^L$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:ceiling}, and $7\le 9=3^2$ gives
$7^L\le (3^L)^2\le n^2$.
\end{proof}

Equivalently $n\ge\sqrt{V/5}$: with $n=5\times 10^4$, no value beyond
$1.25\times 10^{10}$ is ever seen. Note that $\sqrt V$ work to observe a value of
size $V$ is already the trial-division exponent, foreshadowing \S\ref{sec:mt}.

\section{The hypotenuse face is blind to \texorpdfstring{$3\bmod 4$}{3 mod 4} moduli}
\label{sec:hyp}

Call a triple \emph{primitive} if no prime divides both legs.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:legs}
If $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and a prime $r$ divides $a$ and $b$, then $r\mid c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:prim}
Every node of the Berggren tree is a primitive Pythagorean triple.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The root $(3,4,5)$ is primitive. Inductively, suppose $t=(a,b,c)$ is primitive
and Pythagorean and let $r$ divide both legs of $\mathrm{step}_i(t)$. Each
Berggren map is invertible over $\Z$ (determinant $\pm1$), and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:legs} $r$ also divides the child hypotenuse; applying the inverse
integer matrix, $r$ divides $a$ and $b$, contradicting primitivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hypodd}
The hypotenuse of a primitive Pythagorean triple is odd.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $2\mid c$ then $a^2+b^2\equiv 0\pmod 4$; squares are $0$ or $1$ mod $4$, so
$a$ and $b$ are both even, contradicting primitivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hypotenuse primes are $1\bmod 4$]\label{thm:onemod4}
Let $(a,b,c)$ be a primitive Pythagorean triple and $r$ a prime with $r\mid c$.
Then $r\equiv 1\pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:hypodd}, $r$ is odd. Modulo $r$, $a^2+b^2\equiv c^2\equiv 0$.
If $b\equiv 0$ then $a^2\equiv 0$, so $r$ divides both legs, contradicting
primitivity. Hence $b$ is invertible and $(ab^{-1})^2\equiv -1\pmod r$, so $-1$
is a quadratic residue mod $r$, which for odd $r$ holds iff $r\equiv 1\pmod 4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:factorbase}
Every prime divisor of every tree hypotenuse is $\equiv 1\pmod 4$; in particular
no tree hypotenuse is divisible by $2,3,7,11,19,23,\dots$
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:factorbase} explains the measured smoothness advantage:
restricting possible prime divisors to a set of natural density $1/2$, while
excluding the smallest and most common primes $2$ and $3$, changes the constant
substantially but not the exponent. A boost of $7.31\times$ is consistent with a
halved factor base; a naive $44\times$ prediction, which ignores the loss of the
small primes, is not.

\begin{theorem}[Hypotenuse-face blindness]\label{thm:blind1}
Let $N$ be a positive integer all of whose prime divisors are $\equiv 3\pmod 4$.
Then $\gcd(c,N)=1$ for every hypotenuse $c$ of every node of the Berggren tree.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A common prime divisor $r$ would be $\equiv 1\pmod 4$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:onemod4} and $\equiv 3\pmod 4$ by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

This is categorically stronger than the lottery bound: on this class of moduli
the success probability is exactly $0$, uniformly over the whole infinite tree,
at every depth. For contrast, the \emph{leg} face is not blind: the node
$(21,20,29)$ gives $\gcd(21,33)=3$. The obstruction is specific to the
hypotenuse, which is the face carrying the norm-form constraint.

\section{Norm-form blindness in general}

Theorem~\ref{thm:onemod4} used nothing about the tree beyond the shape of its
values: a hypotenuse is a primitively represented value of $x^2+y^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Norm-form constraint]\label{thm:normform}
Let $D,a,b,c\in\Z$ with $a^2+Db^2=c$ and $(a,b)$ primitive. Then for every prime
$r\mid c$, the element $-D$ is a square in $\Z/r\Z$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Modulo $r$, $a^2+Db^2\equiv 0$. If $b\equiv 0$ then $a^2\equiv 0$, so $r$ divides
both $a$ and $b$, contradicting primitivity. So $b$ is invertible and
$(ab^{-1})^2\equiv -D\pmod r$.
\end{proof}

Say a prime $r$ is \emph{inert} for $x^2+Dy^2$ if $-D$ is not a square mod $r$.

\begin{theorem}[Blindness]\label{thm:blindgen}
Let $c=a^2+Db^2$ with $(a,b)$ primitive and let every prime divisor of $N$ be
inert for $x^2+Dy^2$. Then $\gcd(c,N)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[$D=1$]
An odd prime $r$ is inert for $x^2+y^2$ iff $r\equiv 3\pmod 4$. Applying
Theorem~\ref{thm:blindgen} with $a,b$ the legs and $c^2$ the value recovers
Theorem~\ref{thm:blind1}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$D=2$]
An odd prime $r$ is inert for $x^2+2y^2$ iff $r\equiv 5$ or $7\pmod 8$;
equivalently every prime divisor of a primitively represented value of
$x^2+2y^2$ is $2$ or $\equiv 1,3\pmod 8$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Blindness classes are incomparable]\label{thm:incomp}
All of the following hold.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item For every primitive $(a,b)$ and $c=a^2+b^2$: $\gcd(c,3)=1$.
\item There is a primitive $(a,b)$ with $c=a^2+2b^2$ and $\gcd(c,3)=3$, e.g.
      $(1,1)$, $c=3$.
\item For every primitive $(a,b)$ and $c=a^2+2b^2$: $\gcd(c,5)=1$.
\item There is a primitive $(a,b)$ with $c=a^2+b^2$ and $\gcd(c,5)=5$, e.g.
      $(1,2)$, $c=5$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(i) and (iii) are Theorem~\ref{thm:blindgen} with $N=3$, $D=1$ and $N=5$, $D=2$,
using $3\equiv 3\bmod 4$ and $5\equiv 5\bmod 8$; (ii) and (iv) are the exhibited
witnesses.
\end{proof}

Thus changing the norm form \emph{relocates} the obstruction rather than removing
it: every fixed binary quadratic form has an inert class of density $1/2$ among
the primes, and is totally blind to the moduli built from that class.

\begin{theorem}[Infinitely many blind composites]\label{thm:infinite}
The set of composite $N$ all of whose prime factors are $\equiv 3\pmod 4$ is
infinite, as is the set of composite $N$ all of whose prime factors are
$\equiv 5\pmod 8$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Given $B$, Dirichlet's theorem provides a prime $p>B$ with $p\equiv 3\pmod 4$ and
then a prime $q>p$ with $q\equiv 3\pmod 4$; $N=pq$ is composite, exceeds $B$, and
has all prime factors $\equiv 3\bmod 4$. As $B$ was arbitrary the set is
unbounded, hence infinite. The $5\bmod 8$ case is identical.
\end{proof}

\section{The multi-target relaxation is exactly trial division}
\label{sec:mt}

Say $a$ is a \emph{hit} for $N$ if $a\ge 2$ and $\gcd(a,N)>1$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hitlb}
Let $p,q$ be primes and $a$ a hit for $N=pq$. Then $\min(p,q)\le a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(a,pq)>1$ has a prime divisor $r$, with $r\mid a$ and $r\mid pq$; by
Euclid's lemma $r\in\{p,q\}$, so $\min(p,q)\le r$, and $r\mid a$ with $a\ge 2$
gives $r\le a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hitmin}
$\min(p,q)$ is a hit for $pq$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Ascending-sweep first hit]\label{thm:firsthit}
For primes $p,q$,
\[
  \min\{\,a\in\N: a\ge 2,\ \gcd(a,pq)>1\,\}=\min(p,q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:hitmin} gives membership; Lemma~\ref{lem:hitlb} the lower bound.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:firsthit} turns the empirical histogram into a tautology: a
value-guided best-first search whose priority is the node value examines
candidates in ascending order, so its first hit is the least hit, which is
$\min(p,q)$. The observation that $100\%$ of first hits landed at $a=\min(p,q)$
is thus a theorem about the target set, not a statistic about the heuristic.

\begin{theorem}[Cost is the trial-division exponent]\label{thm:cost}
For all $p,q$, $\min(p,q)^2\le pq$, hence $\min(p,q)\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$.
\end{theorem}

The fitted $\alpha=1.087$ with $r^2=1.0$ on the $\log_2$-of-smaller-prime scale
is a perfect linear fit precisely because the relationship is an identity.

\begin{theorem}[Exact speedup]\label{thm:speedup}
$\min(p,q)\cdot\max(p,q)=pq$. Hence the relaxation's speedup over the exact
target $a=N$, in sweep steps, is exactly $\max(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Speedup bounds]\label{thm:speedbounds}
For primes $p,q$: $\lfloor\sqrt{pq}\rfloor\le\max(p,q)$ and $2\max(p,q)\le pq$.
So the speedup factor lies in $[\sqrt N,\,N/2]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$pq\le\max(p,q)^2$ gives the first; $2\le\min(p,q)$ gives
$2\max(p,q)\le\min(p,q)\max(p,q)=pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Balanced case gives no relief]\label{thm:balanced}
If $q\le 2p$ and $p\le 2q$ then $pq\le 2\min(p,q)^2$, i.e.
$\min(p,q)\ge\sqrt{N/2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exponent dominance]\label{thm:expdom}
Let $\alpha<\beta$, $C>0$, $N\ge 1$ real with $N>C^{1/(\beta-\alpha)}$. Then
$C\cdot N^{\alpha}<N^{\beta}$. In particular $C\cdot N^{1/4}<N^{1/2}$ whenever
$N>C^4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$C=(C^{1/(\beta-\alpha)})^{\beta-\alpha}<N^{\beta-\alpha}$, so
$CN^{\alpha}<N^{\beta-\alpha}N^{\alpha}=N^{\beta}$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:expdom} disposes of the constant-factor smoothness advantage of
\S\ref{sec:hyp} as a route to improvement, and places the relaxed search below
Pollard's rho at expected cost $O(N^{1/4})$ --- matching the measured exponents
$\alpha\approx1.087$ against $\alpha=0.458$.

\section{No search order escapes, and both failure modes coincide}

\begin{theorem}[No free lunch for candidate orders]\label{thm:nfl}
Let $f:\N\to\N$ be any $N$-independent enumeration of candidate values, $K$ any
prefix length, $B$ any bound. Then there are primes $p,q$ with $B<p<q$ such that
$\gcd(f(k),pq)=1$ for all $k<K$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $M=\max(B,\max_{k<K}f(k))$, finite. Choose a prime $p>M$ and then a prime
$q>p$. If some $f(k)$ with $k<K$ were a hit for $pq$, Lemma~\ref{lem:hitlb} gives
$p=\min(p,q)\le f(k)\le M<p$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

A finite $N$-independent prefix is a finite set of integers, and a finite set of
integers exposes only the primes below its maximum. Combined with
Theorem~\ref{thm:firsthit}, no reordering avoids a cost growing with $\min(p,q)$.

\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous defeat]\label{thm:both}
Let $f:\N\to\N$ be any enumeration, $K$ any prefix length, $B$ any bound. Then
there are primes $p,q$ with $B<p<q$ and $p\equiv q\equiv 3\pmod 4$ such that
$\gcd(f(k),pq)=1$ for all $k<K$, and $\gcd(c^2,pq)=1$ for every hypotenuse $c$ of
every node of the Berggren tree.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $M=\max(B,\max_{k<K}f(k))$ and, by Dirichlet, choose primes $p>M$ and $q>p$
with $p\equiv q\equiv 3\pmod 4$. The first claim is the argument of
Theorem~\ref{thm:nfl}; the second is Theorem~\ref{thm:blind1} applied to $N=pq$.
\end{proof}

\section{The trichotomy, and the positive half}
\label{sec:tri}

First, the positive half must be non-vacuous.

\begin{theorem}[Explicit nontrivial square root of unity]\label{thm:sqrt1}
Let $p\ne q$ be distinct odd primes and let $u,v\in\Z$ satisfy $up+vq=1$. Put
$z=1-2up$. Then $pq\mid(z-1)(z+1)$, $pq\nmid z-1$, $pq\nmid z+1$; more precisely
$p\mid z-1$ with $q\nmid z-1$, and $q\mid z+1$ with $p\nmid z+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$z-1=-2up$ and $z+1=2-2up=2vq$, so their product is $-4uvpq$. If $q\mid up$ then
$q\mid up+vq=1$, impossible; and $q\nmid 2$; hence $q\nmid z-1$. Symmetrically
$p\nmid z+1$. Divisibility of either factor by $pq$ would force the missing
divisibility.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact gcds; yield $1$]\label{thm:yield}
With $z$ as above, $\gcd(z-1,pq)=p$, $\gcd(z+1,pq)=q$, and the two gcds multiply
back to $pq$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(z-1,pq)$ divides $pq$, is divisible by $p$, and is not divisible by $q$, so
equals $p$; symmetrically for $z+1$.
\end{proof}

Operationally: once a nontrivial square root of unity is in hand the
factorisation is recovered completely, with yield $1$. All the cost of a
Dixon-class method therefore sits in \emph{producing} the relation, none in
exploiting it --- which is exactly why a tree offering relations but not
smoothness offers nothing to the quadratic-sieve family.

\begin{definition}[Regimes]\label{def:regimes}
Fix $N$. A splitter is in regime
\textbf{(I) integer identity} if its relation is $X^2=Y^2$ with $X,Y\ge0$ in
$\Z$;
\textbf{(II) Dixon} if its relation is $N\mid(x-y)(x+y)$ with $N\nmid x-y$ and
$N\nmid x+y$;
\textbf{(III) ascending sweep} if it examines candidate values in ascending order
and stops at the first hit.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Trichotomy for the integer face]\label{thm:trichotomy}
Let $N=pq>1$ with $p,q$ prime. Then in regime (I), $\gcd(X-Y,N)=N$: the output is
$N$ itself, no split. In regime (II), $1<\gcd(x-y,N)<N$: a proper nontrivial
factor, the Dixon/quadratic-sieve outcome. In regime (III), the first hit is the
least element of the hit set and equals $\min(p,q)$, with $\min(p,q)^2\le N$:
trial division.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Regime (I) is Theorem~\ref{thm:vacuous}; regime (II) is Theorem~\ref{thm:dixon};
regime (III) is Theorems~\ref{thm:firsthit} and \ref{thm:cost}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
It is not enough to prove a \emph{disjunction} of three existential statements,
since a disjunction can be discharged by an unrelated witness. The statement
above attaches to each regime \emph{its own} outcome, computed from the data that
regime supplies: the identity's $X,Y$; the congruence's $x,y$; the sweep's
stopping index. In that sharp form the outcome is determined by the regime.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
No splitter reading integers off the Berggren tree improves on textbook methods.
Regime (I) never splits; regime (II) is Dixon/QS with cost governed by
smoothness, to which the tree contributes only a bounded constant
(Theorem~\ref{thm:expdom}); regime (III) is trial division with cost
$\min(p,q)\le\sqrt N$, dominated by Pollard's rho at $O(N^{1/4})$ for all
sufficiently large $N$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

Three algorithmic objects are implicit in the analysis.

\paragraph{A. Berggren enumeration with hypotenuse residue audit.} Enumerate tree
nodes from $(3,4,5)$ via the three transformations, recording each hypotenuse and
its factorisation; verify every prime factor is $\equiv 1\bmod 4$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:onemod4}) and report the empirical smoothness rate against
random integers of matching bit length. Complexity: $\Theta(3^L)$ nodes to depth
$L$, values bounded by $5\cdot7^L$, hence value ceiling $5n^2$ for $n$ nodes.

\paragraph{B. Lottery bound evaluator.} Given $N$-independent outputs
$D_0,\dots,D_{k-1}$ and a prime pool $S$, compute the winning set
$\{r\in S:\exists i,\ r\mid D_i\}$ and compare against the certified bound
$\sum_i\log_2 D_i$. Complexity: $O(k|S|)$ divisibility tests.

\paragraph{C. Ascending sweep with first-hit certification.} Scan
$a=2,3,4,\dots$ computing $\gcd(a,N)$, halting at the first hit; certify that it
equals $\min(p,q)$ and that $a^2\le N$. Complexity: exactly $\min(p,q)-1$ gcd
computations, i.e.\ $O(\sqrt N\log^2 N)$ bit operations --- trial division. The
salient point is that Algorithm C, which is what the value-guided best-first
search reduces to, has a closed-form cost; there is no heuristic to tune.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why the smoothness boost cannot help}
Structured search spaces are attractive because their values are arithmetically
special, and arithmetically special values are smoother:
Theorem~\ref{thm:onemod4} explains the measured $7.31\times$ boost exactly. But
the same restriction is a support constraint, and a support constraint is a blind
spot. The set of primes that can divide your values and the set that cannot are
complements; making the first smaller --- which buys smoothness --- makes the
second bigger, and Theorem~\ref{thm:blindgen} says the second is a total
obstruction, not a probabilistic one. Quantitatively the trade is hopeless: the
gain is a bounded constant, killed by Theorem~\ref{thm:expdom}, while the loss is
an infinite family of moduli on which the method has probability exactly $0$.

\subsection{Where the $10^{12}$ speedup went}
By every operational criterion the relaxation worked: $1500/1500$ paired wins,
median visit ratio $0.111$, zero censoring against $55\%$, effective cost from
${\sim}2^{56}$ to ${\sim}2^{16}$. Nothing about those numbers is wrong. What they
measure is improvement relative to a baseline, not the identity of the resulting
algorithm. Theorem~\ref{thm:speedup} says the improvement is exactly $\max(p,q)$,
and Theorems~\ref{thm:firsthit}--\ref{thm:cost} say what it improves \emph{to} is
the ascending sweep at cost $\min(p,q)\le\sqrt N$. A $10^{12}$ speedup that
terminates at trial division is a $10^{12}$ speedup, and it is trial division.
The methodological lesson generalises: a paired benchmark answers ``did we
improve?'', but only the distribution of \emph{solutions found} answers ``what
did we build?''. Here that distribution was a single spike at $\min(p,q)$, and
the spike was a theorem.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}
Our results concern splitters that read integer values off the tree and conclude
with a gcd. They do not by themselves rule out using tree relations only as a
source of smooth relations inside a quadratic sieve; Theorem~\ref{thm:onemod4}
remains relevant there (the factor base is halved, an advantage for smoothness
and a disadvantage for coverage), but such a hybrid would require accounting for
relation-collection cost against standard sieving, and Theorem~\ref{thm:expdom}
suggests the outlook is poor. Similarly, Theorem~\ref{thm:nfl} concerns
$N$-independent enumerations; enumerations that adapt to $N$ --- as Pollard's rho
does, by iterating a map modulo $N$ --- are outside its scope, which is precisely
the point: adaptivity to the modulus is where exponent $1/4$ comes from, and it
is the ingredient the tree proposals lack.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Other forms and other trees.} Theorem~\ref{thm:blindgen} applies to any
search whose values are primitively represented by $x^2+Dy^2$. Tabulating blind
classes for a range of $D$, and for non-principal forms of a given discriminant
where representation is by a genus rather than a single form, would give a
screening test for structured-search factoring proposals. The $D=1$ and $D=2$
classes are incomparable (Theorem~\ref{thm:incomp}), raising the question whether
a family of forms run in parallel can cover all primes --- which collides with
Theorem~\ref{thm:union}: running $m$ forms multiplies cost by $m$ while the
lottery bound adds only linearly.

\paragraph{Densities.} Theorem~\ref{thm:infinite} shows the blind set is
infinite; making ``positive density of moduli on which the method has probability
zero'' fully quantitative is a natural sharpening.

\paragraph{Adaptive orders.} Theorem~\ref{thm:nfl} rules out $N$-independent
enumerations. What is the analogue for enumerations that may query $N$ a bounded
number of times, or use $O(\log N)$ bits of $N$? This would formally separate the
tree family from rho.

\paragraph{Sharper starvation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:starve} uses only $7\le 3^2$.
The three branches have unequal growth rates, so a branch-dependent analysis
should give a better constant and possibly a better exponent for depth-first or
value-priority exploration, which is what value-guided search actually performs.

\section{Conclusion}

The Berggren tree is a beautiful object, and it is not a factoring algorithm. The
proposal that harvests square relations from it is invalid as stated, because its
relations live in $\Z$ and never meet the modulus. Weakened to a gcd lottery, it
wins at exactly the rate that linearly-adding, $N$-independent tickets predict.
Explored breadth-first, it never reaches interesting values. Restricted, as it
must be, to values primitively represented by $x^2+y^2$, it is totally blind to
an infinite class of moduli --- the same restriction that gives it its real but
bounded smoothness advantage. Relaxed to a multi-target search, it accelerates by
a factor of exactly $\max(p,q)$ and arrives, deterministically and provably, at
trial division. Every route through the tree's integer face terminates in a
method that was already in the textbooks, and the terminus is determined by which
of three regimes the relation occupies.

